# Stays, in the order a reader would try it. Start the stack (docker compose up -d --wait) and open this file in
# VS Code with the REST Client extension, or in a JetBrains IDE. Every request carries api-version.
#
# The people are in the Keycloak realm: partner-stays1 owns properties, traveller1 and admin1 are what they sound
# like. Their password is "viatica". A second partner for the cross-tenant requests is partner-cars1, whose token
# also carries a partnerId (its audience is the same, so Stays accepts it and answers 404 for what is not theirs).

@stays = http://localhost:8101
@keycloak = http://localhost:8080/realms/viatica/protocol/openid-connect/token
@v = api-version=2026-11-01

# Ids are chosen by the client, so every creation below can be sent twice. Change them for a fresh property.
@propertyId = 0192f0c4-7a3b-7cde-8f00-4f7c2b1d9e01
@roomTypeId = 0192f0c4-7a3b-7cde-8f00-4f7c2b1d9e02

### 1. Sign in as the partner. Later requests read the token from here.
# @name partnerLogin
POST {{keycloak}}
Content-Type: application/x-www-form-urlencoded

grant_type=password&client_id=viatica-cli&username=partner-stays1&password=viatica

@partnerToken = {{partnerLogin.response.body.access_token}}

### 2. Create a property. PUT with your own id: sending it again returns the same property (200 instead of 201).
# It starts as a Draft and only you and an admin can see it. The ETag in the answer is what later updates need.
PUT {{stays}}/properties/{{propertyId}}?{{v}}
Authorization: Bearer {{partnerToken}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "name": "Casa do Mar",
  "description": "Twelve rooms above the harbour in Alfama.",
  "address": { "line1": "Rua das Flores 12", "city": "Lisbon", "region": "Lisboa", "postalCode": "1200-195", "countryCode": "PT" },
  "location": { "latitude": 38.7139, "longitude": -9.1334 },
  "starRating": 4,
  "amenities": ["wifi", "breakfast", "rooftop pool"],
  "checkInFrom": "15:00:00",
  "checkOutUntil": "11:00:00",
  "cancellationPolicy": { "freeUntilHoursBeforeStart": 48, "penaltyAfter": "FirstUnit" }
}

### 3. Read it back (the ETag header is the current version).
# @name property
GET {{stays}}/properties/{{propertyId}}?{{v}}
Authorization: Bearer {{partnerToken}}

### 4. Change it. PATCH takes only the fields you want to change and needs If-Match with the ETag from step 3.
# Without If-Match: 428. With an old ETag: 412. Copy the ETag here.
PATCH {{stays}}/properties/{{propertyId}}?{{v}}
Authorization: Bearer {{partnerToken}}
Content-Type: application/json
If-Match: "REPLACE-WITH-THE-ETAG"

{ "description": "Twelve rooms above the harbour in Alfama, a minute from the tram." }

### 5. Add a room type. Same rules: your own id, PUT to create, PATCH with If-Match to change.
# baseRate is what a night costs when a calendar edit does not name a rate.
PUT {{stays}}/properties/{{propertyId}}/room-types/{{roomTypeId}}?{{v}}
Authorization: Bearer {{partnerToken}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "name": "Double with balcony",
  "maxOccupancy": 2,
  "beds": "1 double",
  "amenities": ["balcony", "air conditioning"],
  "baseRate": { "amount": "120.00", "currency": "EUR" }
}

### 6. Put nights on sale: 8 rooms a night for all of December, 140 EUR. One request, one transaction, any range up to
# a year ahead. Repeatability-Request-ID makes a retry safe: send this twice and the second answer is the first one.
# Repeatability-First-Sent must be recent; the REST Client fills {{$datetime}} for you.
POST {{stays}}/properties/{{propertyId}}/room-types/{{roomTypeId}}/inventory:set?{{v}}
Authorization: Bearer {{partnerToken}}
Content-Type: application/json
Repeatability-Request-ID: 6f0c1d0e-2b7a-4c52-9d6e-0a1b2c3d4e5f
Repeatability-First-Sent: {{$datetime rfc1123}}

{ "from": "2026-12-01", "to": "2026-12-31", "allotment": 8, "rate": { "amount": "140.00", "currency": "EUR" } }

### 7. Look at the calendar: allotment, sold, held and rate per night.
GET {{stays}}/properties/{{propertyId}}/room-types/{{roomTypeId}}/inventory?from=2026-12-01&to=2026-12-07&{{v}}
Authorization: Bearer {{partnerToken}}

### 8. Publish. Needs a room type, needs If-Match. This is when search learns about the property.
POST {{stays}}/properties/{{propertyId}}/publish?{{v}}
Authorization: Bearer {{partnerToken}}
If-Match: "REPLACE-WITH-THE-ETAG"

### 9. Anyone can now read it, no token. A draft would be a 404.
GET {{stays}}/properties/{{propertyId}}?{{v}}

### 10. The public list: published properties only, 20 a page, nextLink to follow. $filter and $orderby are allowlisted.
GET {{stays}}/properties?$filter=starRating ge 4&$orderby=name&$top=20&{{v}}

### 11. The partner's own list, drafts included.
GET {{stays}}/partner/properties?{{v}}
Authorization: Bearer {{partnerToken}}

### 12. A quote, no sign in needed: the nightly breakdown, the total, and a token that pins the price for 15 minutes.
# Booking hands the token back when it asks for a hold. The token is readable (it is base64url JSON before the dot) but
# only Stays can mint one.
# @name quote
POST {{stays}}/properties/{{propertyId}}/room-types/{{roomTypeId}}/quotes?{{v}}
Content-Type: application/json

{ "checkIn": "2026-12-01", "checkOut": "2026-12-04", "quantity": 1, "guests": 2 }

### 13. What a stay refused looks like: 31 nights is StayTooLong, 3 guests in a double is OccupancyExceeded.
POST {{stays}}/properties/{{propertyId}}/room-types/{{roomTypeId}}/quotes?{{v}}
Content-Type: application/json

{ "checkIn": "2026-12-01", "checkOut": "2027-01-01", "quantity": 1, "guests": 3 }

### 14. Take it off sale. The partner may, and so may an admin.
POST {{stays}}/properties/{{propertyId}}/unpublish?{{v}}
Authorization: Bearer {{partnerToken}}
If-Match: "REPLACE-WITH-THE-ETAG"

### 15. Another partner's token on this property answers 404, never 403, so ids do not leak.
# @name otherLogin
POST {{keycloak}}
Content-Type: application/x-www-form-urlencoded

grant_type=password&client_id=viatica-cli&username=partner-cars1&password=viatica

###
PATCH {{stays}}/properties/{{propertyId}}?{{v}}
Authorization: Bearer {{otherLogin.response.body.access_token}}
Content-Type: application/json
If-Match: *

{ "name": "Mine now" }

### Holds are not an HTTP endpoint. Booking asks for them with StayHoldRequested on booking.v1, and Stays answers on
# stays.v1; see docs/services/stays.md for the conversation.
